<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-epidemic-sage" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Epidemic Dynamics</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this section we model the spread of a disease through a population as a Markov
      chain, and we use the computer algebra system
      <url href="https://www.sagemath.org" visual="sagemath.org">Sage</url> to simulate
      the model and plot how the population evolves over time. In the process we will
      revisit the main themes of this chapter: transition matrices, stochastic matrices,
      steady states, and eigenvalues.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-epidemic-model">
    <title>The Model</title>

    <p>
      A disease is introduced into a population. In each period (say, days) we count the
      fraction of the population that is in four different infection states:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Susceptible.</term> These individuals can acquire the disease the next
            day.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><term>Infected.</term> These individuals have the disease.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Recovered</term> (and immune). These individuals had the disease and
            survived, and now have immunity.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Deceased.</term> These individuals had the disease, and unfortunately
            died from it.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>s_k</m>, <m>i_k</m>, <m>r_k</m>, and <m>d_k</m> be the fractions of the
      population in each of the four states on day <m>k</m>, and collect them into the
      state vector
      <md>
        \vec{S}_k = \begin{bmatrix} s_k \\ i_k \\ r_k \\ d_k \end{bmatrix} \in \R^4.
      </md>
      For example,
      <m>\vec{S}_k = \begin{bmatrix} 0.75 \\ 0.10 \\ 0.10 \\ 0.05 \end{bmatrix}</m>
      means that on day <m>k</m>, <m>75\%</m> of the population is susceptible,
      <m>10\%</m> is infected, <m>10\%</m> is recovered and immune, and <m>5\%</m> has
      died from the disease. Note that the entries of <m>\vec{S}_k</m> are nonnegative
      and add up to one, so <m>\vec{S}_k</m> is a probability vector.
    </p>

    <p>
      Our model assumes the following happens over each day.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>5\%</m> of the susceptible population will acquire the disease. (The
            other <m>95\%</m> will remain susceptible.)
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>1\%</m> of the infected population will die from the disease, <m>10\%</m>
            will recover and acquire immunity, and <m>4\%</m> will recover and not
            acquire immunity (and therefore, become susceptible). The remaining
            <m>85\%</m> will remain infected.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Those who have recovered with immunity and those who have died remain in
            those states.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Let us translate these rules into equations. Tomorrow's susceptible individuals
      are today's susceptible individuals who did not become infected, which is
      <m>0.95\,s_k</m>, plus today's infected individuals who recovered without
      immunity, which is <m>0.04\,i_k</m>. Tomorrow's infected individuals are those
      who remain infected, <m>0.85\,i_k</m>, plus the susceptible individuals who
      acquire the disease, <m>0.05\,s_k</m>. Similar arguments for the last two states
      give the system
      <md>
        <mrow>s_{k+1} \amp = 0.95\,s_k + 0.04\,i_k,</mrow>
        <mrow>i_{k+1} \amp = 0.05\,s_k + 0.85\,i_k,</mrow>
        <mrow>r_{k+1} \amp = r_k + 0.10\,i_k,</mrow>
        <mrow>d_{k+1} \amp = d_k + 0.01\,i_k.</mrow>
      </md>
      We put these together in matrix form as <m>\vec{S}_{k+1} = T\vec{S}_k</m>, where
      the transition matrix is
      <md>
        T = \begin{bmatrix}
          0.95 \amp 0.04 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
          0.05 \amp 0.85 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 0.10 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 0.01 \amp 0 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Each column of <m>T</m> is a probability vector, so <m>T</m> is a stochastic
      matrix as in <xref ref="def-stochastic-matrix" text="type-global"/>. We will
      simulate the model starting from
      <m>\vec{S}_0 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>: everyone is
      susceptible, and the disease is about to be introduced. Iterating
      <m>\vec{S}_{k+1} = T\vec{S}_k</m> by hand for hundreds of days is hopeless, so we
      hand the iteration to Sage.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-epidemic-sage-setup">
    <title>Setting Up the Model in Sage</title>

    <p>
      Each block of code below is an interactive <term>Sage cell</term>: press
      <term>Evaluate</term> to run it in your browser. (You can also copy the code into
      <url href="https://sagecell.sagemath.org" visual="sagecell.sagemath.org">the Sage
      cell server</url> or a CoCalc worksheet.) Within a single page the cells share
      their variables, but they must be evaluated in order, so run each cell as you
      reach it.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Step 1: enter <m>T</m> and <m>\vec{S}_0</m>.</term>
      In Sage, <c>matrix(QQ, [...])</c> builds a matrix from a list of rows, and
      <c>vector(QQ, [...])</c> builds a vector. The ring <c>QQ</c> tells Sage to store
      the entries as exact rational numbers, so no rounding errors accumulate during
      the iteration.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        T = matrix(QQ, [[0.95, 0.04, 0, 0],
                        [0.05, 0.85, 0, 0],
                        [0,    0.10, 1, 0],
                        [0,    0.01, 0, 1]])
        S0 = vector(QQ, [1, 0, 0, 0])
        T
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      <term>Step 2: check that <m>T</m> is stochastic.</term>
      Before simulating, it is good practice to make sure we typed the matrix
      correctly. The command below computes the sum of the entries of each column of
      <m>T</m>; every sum should equal one.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        [sum(col) for col in T.columns()]
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      <term>Step 3: compute the first few days.</term>
      Matrix-vector multiplication in Sage is written <c>T*S</c>, so one day of the
      epidemic is one multiplication. The loop below computes
      <m>\vec{S}_1, \ldots, \vec{S}_5</m>; the method <c>.n(digits=4)</c> displays each
      exact answer as a decimal with four significant digits.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        S = S0
        for k in range(1, 6):
            S = T*S
            print(k, S.n(digits=4))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      Check the first line against the model: after one day we expect
      <m>\vec{S}_1 = T\vec{S}_0 = \begin{bmatrix} 0.95 \\ 0.05 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
      since on day zero there is nobody infected yet, and <m>5\%</m> of the susceptible
      population acquires the disease.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-epidemic-sage-plot">
    <title>Simulating and Plotting the Epidemic</title>

    <p>
      <term>Step 4: simulate <m>200</m> days and plot the four fractions.</term>
      To draw the graphs we store the whole history of the simulation: the list
      <c>history</c> holds <m>\vec{S}_0, \vec{S}_1, \ldots, \vec{S}_{200}</m>, where
      each new state is obtained by multiplying the previous one
      (<c>history[-1]</c> is the last entry of the list) by <m>T</m>. Then, for each
      state <m>j = 1, 2, 3, 4</m>, the command <c>line</c> plots the points
      <m>\left( k, (\vec{S}_k)_j \right)</m> for <m>k = 0, 1, \ldots, 200</m> and
      connects them with a curve. (In Sage, list and vector indices start at zero, so
      the entry <m>(\vec{S}_k)_j</m> is written <c>history[k][j-1]</c>; the loop
      variable <c>j</c> below runs through <m>0, 1, 2, 3</m>.) Adding the four plots
      combines them into a single figure with a legend.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        N = 200
        history = [S0]
        for k in range(N):
            history.append(T * history[-1])

        labels = ["susceptible", "infected", "recovered", "deceased"]
        colors = ["blue", "red", "green", "black"]
        p = sum(line([(k, history[k][j]) for k in range(N + 1)],
                     color=colors[j], legend_label=labels[j], thickness=2)
                for j in range(4))
        p.axes_labels(["day $k$", "fraction of population"])
        p.show(ymin=0, ymax=1)
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      You should see the susceptible fraction decay from one towards zero, the infected
      fraction rise to a peak of roughly <m>21\%</m> around day <m>11</m> and then die
      out, and the recovered and deceased fractions climb towards limiting values. The
      simulation shows that after around <m>100</m> days, the state converges to one
      with a little under <m>10\%</m> of the population deceased, and the remaining
      population immune.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-epidemic-long-run">
    <title>The Long Run</title>

    <p>
      <term>Step 5: where does the epidemic end?</term>
      The plot suggests that the state vectors converge. To see the limit numerically,
      ask Sage for a state far in the future, say
      <m>\vec{S}_{1000} = T^{1000}\,\vec{S}_0</m>.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        (T^1000 * S0).n(digits=4)
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      So in the long run about <m>90.9\%</m> of the population ends up recovered and
      immune, and about <m>9.1\%</m> deceased. (In <xref ref="exercises-epidemic-sage"
      text="type-global"/> you will show that the exact limits are
      <m>\frac{10}{11}</m> and <m>\frac{1}{11}</m>.)
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Step 6: compare with the eigenvalue point of view.</term>
      By <xref ref="eg-stochastic-eigenvalue-one" text="type-global"/>, the stochastic
      matrix <m>T</m> must have eigenvalue one. Let us ask Sage for all the eigenvalues
      and eigenvectors of <m>T</m>. The command below returns a list of triples
      <c>(eigenvalue, [eigenvectors], multiplicity)</c>.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        T.eigenvectors_right()
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      Sage reports the eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 1</m> with multiplicity <em>two</em>,
      together with the two independent eigenvectors
      <md>
        \vec{x}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        \vec{x}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
      </md>
      and two further eigenvalues with absolute value less than one. This is consistent
      with what the simulation showed: the states <q>everyone recovered</q> and
      <q>everyone deceased</q> are both fixed by <m>T</m>, and so is any probability
      vector <m>a\vec{x}_1 + b\vec{x}_2</m> built from them. The epidemic therefore has
      infinitely many steady states, and which one the population converges to depends
      on the initial state <m>\vec{S}_0</m>.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-epidemic-not-regular">
      <title>Why the Steady State Is Not Unique</title>
      <p>
        Does this contradict <xref ref="thm-unique-steady-state" text="type-global"/>?
        No: that theorem assumes the transition matrix is <em>regular</em>, and
        <m>T</m> is not. However high the power <m>T^k</m>, its third and fourth
        columns remain <m>\vec{x}_1</m> and <m>\vec{x}_2</m> (recovered and deceased
        individuals never leave their states), so <m>T^k</m> always has zero entries.
        You can confirm this in Sage by evaluating, say, <c>T^50</c>.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-epidemic-sage">

    <exercise xml:id="exer-epidemic-initial-state">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rerun the simulation of <xref ref="subsec-epidemic-sage-plot"
          text="type-global"/> with the initial state
          <m>\vec{S}_0 = \begin{bmatrix} 0.75 \\ 0.10 \\ 0.10 \\ 0.05 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Do the state vectors still converge? Do they converge to the same limit as
          before? Explain how your answer is consistent with
          <xref ref="rem-epidemic-not-regular" text="type-global"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-epidemic-vaccination">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose a vaccine becomes available, and each day <m>2\%</m> of the
          susceptible population is vaccinated, acquiring immunity without ever being
          infected (so vaccinated individuals move directly from susceptible to
          recovered). Modify the equations of the model, write down the new transition
          matrix, and adapt your Sage simulation. How does the graph change, and what
          fraction of the population is deceased in the long run?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-epidemic-exact-limit">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that with <m>\vec{S}_0 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          the deceased fraction converges to exactly <m>\frac{1}{11}</m>, and the
          recovered fraction to exactly <m>\frac{10}{11}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Each day, <m>0.01\,i_k</m> is added to the deceased fraction and
          <m>0.10\,i_k</m> to the recovered fraction, so their limits are
          <m>0.01\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} i_k</m> and <m>0.10\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} i_k</m> in
          the ratio <m>1 : 10</m>. Since eventually everyone is either recovered or
          deceased, the two limits add up to one.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-epidemic-peak">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use your <c>history</c> list from the simulation to find the day on which the
          infected fraction peaks, and the value of the peak. (Try
          <c>max((history[k][1], k) for k in range(N + 1))</c>, which compares the
          pairs <m>\left( i_k, k \right)</m>.)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </exercises>
</section>
